cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Single Human-AI Fit: One Attribute, Seven Channels, Two Directions
message: If you use this work, please cite it as below.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Podgortsev
    given-names: Michael
    email: podgortsev@gmail.com
repository-code: https://github.com/podgortsev/Single-Human-AI-Fit
license: MIT
date-released: '2026-09-03'
keywords:
  - fairness
  - large language models
  - counterfactual audit
  - disability
  - ageism
  - accessibility
  - evaluation methodology
  - negative results
abstract: >-
  Seven measurement methods applied to three open language models
  (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.1-8B, Mistral-7B-v0.3) to test whether a model
  treats a person differently when one detail about them changes and
  everything else stays byte-identical. The four disclosures tested are
  screen reader use, Deafness, ADHD and age 74.

  Four behavioural channels agree: disclosure is associated with fewer
  favourable trait words, lower accuracy on the person's own arithmetic
  task, a worse open-ended answer, and losing a head-to-head choice
  against a socially neutral alternative detail. A fifth channel
  disagrees: asked for a numeric score about the person, the models rate
  Deafness and screen reader use higher. The model rates the disclosed
  person better in isolation and treats them worse in behaviour, which
  matters because the score is the measure most audits use.

  Two results are negative and reported in full: signals do not
  accumulate, and answers are not less stable for people who disclose.
  A forced-choice design failed outright because position bias did not
  cancel. Models can admit in aggregate that a disclosure affected them,
  and on one model they over-claim it, but their self-report does not
  correlate with which items actually changed in any of nine cells.

  All datasets, raw model outputs, analysis scripts and offline
  validators are included; every reported figure regenerates without a
  GPU.
